===Etymology 1===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*mon-}}
{{etymon|en|:inh|enm:man}}
From {{inh|en|enm|man}}, from {{inh|en|ang|mann|g=m|t=human being, person, man}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*mann}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*mann-|t=man}}, from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*mon-|t=human being, man}}. {{doublet|en|mann|Manu}}.

{{col-top|1|cog}}
This word has many {{lg|cognate}}s, in every Germanic language (whether extant or extinct), with similarities in form and meaning.<br/>
Virtually all the words below mean “man” and “husband”, with some specific cases noted.

In {{w|Ingvaeonic}} (the languages closest to English):
:* {{cog|nds|Mann}}
:* {{cog|frr|maan,man,moon,muon}}
:* {{cog|stq|Mon}}
:* {{cog|sco|man}}
:* {{cog|fy|man}}
:* {{cog|yol|man}}

In the remainder of West Germanic:
:* {{cog|gsw|ma,Maa,Mann,mo,mà,Mànn}}
:* {{cog|bar|Mo,mon,moon,Må,mònn}}
:* {{cog|cim|man,mann,månn}}
:* {{cog|nl,li,zea|man}}
:* {{cog|de,lb|Mann}}
:* {{cog|mhn|mònn}}
:* {{cog|wym|maon,mon,mōn}}
:* {{cog|yi|מאַן}}

In East Germanic:
:* {{cog|got|𐌼𐌰𐌽𐌽𐌰}}, which meant “man”, “human“ and “person”, but likely also “husband”

In North Germanic:
:* {{cog|da|mand}}
:* {{cog|fo,is|maður}}, both additionally meaning “human” and “person”, as in Old Norse
:* {{cog|gmq-gut,sv|man}}
:* {{cog|nrn,nb,nn|mann}}
:* {{cog|nn|mainnj}}
:* {{cog|non|maðr}}
<!--Do not add Russenorsk мань (manʹ), which you can see in the Proto-Germanic page-->
