==Dutch==
{{wp|nl:}}

===Etymology===
{{ety|nl|:inh|dum:man}}
From {{inh|nl|dum|man}}, from {{inh|nl|odt|man}}, from {{inh|nl|gmw-pro|*mann}}, from {{inh|nl|gem-pro|*mann-}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|nl|/mɑn/}}
* {{audio|nl|Nl-man.ogg}}
* {{hyph|nl|man}}
* {{rhymes|nl|ɑn|s=1}}

===Noun===
{{nl-noun|m|-en,man,-s|+,-neke,-neken,-nechie}}

# {{l|en|man}}, human [[male]], either adult or age-irrespective
#: {{uxi|nl|De '''man''' liep rustig door het park.|The '''man''' walked calmly through the park.}}
#: {{uxi|nl|De jonge '''mannen''' speelden voetbal op het veld.|The young '''men''' were playing soccer on the field.}}
#: {{uxi|nl|De oudere '''man''' glimlachte vriendelijk naar de kinderen.|The older '''man''' smiled kindly at the children.}}
# [[husband]], male [[spouse]]
#: {{uxi|nl|Ze is al jaren gelukkig getrouwd met haar '''man'''.|She has been happily married to her '''husband''' for years.}}
#: {{uxi|nl|Hij is een zorgzame '''man''' en een geweldige vader.|He is a caring '''husband''' and a great father.}}
#: {{uxi|nl|Haar '''man''' verraste haar met een romantisch diner.|Her '''husband''' surprised her with a romantic dinner.}}

====Usage notes====
* The normal plural is ''mannen''. The unchanged form ''man'' is used after numerals only; it refers to the size of a group rather than a number of individuals. For example: ''In totaal verloren er 5000 <u>man</u> hun leven in die slag.'' (“5000 <u>men</u> altogether lost their lives in that battle.”) The plural ''mans'' is dated, now mostly occurring in nautical contexts or in dialect.
* Compound words with ''-man'' as their last component often take -{{m|nl|lieden}} or -{{m|nl|lui}} in the plural, rather than ''-mannen''. For example: {{m|nl|brandweerman||firefighter}} → {{m|nl|brandweerlieden}} (alongside {{m|nl|brandweerlui}} and {{m|nl|brandweermannen}}).
* Various alternative diminutives exist, including {{m|nl|manneke}} (used especially in Flanders) and the dialectal {{m|nl|mannechie}} / {{m|nl|mennechie}}.

